<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matignon Accords (1988)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1988</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Matignon Accords were signed in June 1988 at the Hôtel Matignon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by the French government of Michel Rocard, the Kanak independence leader Jean-Marie Tjibaou and the loyalist leader Jacques Lafleur. Ending years of violent conflict in New Caledonia, they divided the territory into three provinces, provided for development of Kanak areas and promised a referendum on independence after ten years.</p></div>
